2. Operational Overview Dashboard 1 (Tableau)
> Software Used
Created in Tableau, chosen for its interactive capabilities, geospatial mapping, and KPI integration.
> Intended Audience
Healthcare administrators, financial analysts, and policy strategists analyzing performance and resource use across the city.
> Message Conveyed
Summarizes 3-year hospital performance: average patient age, length of stay (LOS), complication counts, and costs. The map shows geographic disparities in total charges, and bar-line graphs link cost brackets to LOS.
> Design Considerations
Color Scheme: Green tones for charges and LOS consistency
Map: Spatial analysis of regional cost burdens
Combo Graph: Illustrates how higher costs relate to increased LOS
Top-Level KPIs: Placed for quick executive scanning
> Reproducibility
Fully filterable by date and payer group. Tableau dashboards can be saved with embedded data, making them reproducible if shared.
> Accessibility
High-contrast visuals and legible fonts help with clarity. However, Tableau’s native outputs may need additional work (e.g., alt-text) to meet screen reader accessibility standards.
> Impacted Communities
Patients across Toronto’s regions, particularly where costs are higher. Payers like OHIP and private insurers also benefit from insights.
> Feature Selection
Selected direct operational KPIs: total charges, LOS, age, and regional data. Excluded provider-level granularity for dashboard simplicity.
> Underwater Labour
Data cleaning and aggregation
Region mapping with GIS layers
Creating calculated KPIs (e.g., avg. charges per encounter)

Clinical Quality & Performance Dashboard 2
> Software Used
Built in Tableau, which supports clinical benchmarking and multidimensional data views.
> Intended Audience
Quality improvement officers, clinical leadership, and safety teams interested in tracking complications and readmissions.
> Message Conveyed
Compares observed vs. expected complications across service lines and visualizes trends in readmission rates and length of stay. Highlights the top 10 service lines contributing to readmissions.
> Design Considerations
Bar Graphs: Used to compare observed vs. expected outcomes
Color Coding: Yellow for expected, green for observed—assisting visual benchmarking
Trend Lines: Display changes in complications and readmissions over time
> Reproducibility
Interactive filters make comparisons flexible. Tableau workbooks preserve structure, ensuring the analysis can be revisited and replicated.
> Accessibility
High-contrast bars and trend lines are helpful, though tooltips and supporting text explanations would improve usability for screen readers.
> Impacted Communities
Physician groups being benchmarked, departments with high complication rates, and patients treated in higher-risk service lines.
> Feature Selection
Focused on top contributors to readmissions and departments with measurable complication rates. Avoided over-segmentation to keep the dashboard actionable.
